{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Like streams they stretch out, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 105b) expounded many interpretations of this. And it is possible to explain the verse in this manner: that the verse speaks concerning four classes of the righteous in the nation of HaShem. The first is Samuel the prophet, who would circuit the entire land of Israel to teach Israel Torah and commandments every single year, as they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 56a). The second is the class of the righteous who are fixed in Torah study — to learn and to teach and to instruct in judgment — and this is the class that stands in the Chamber of Hewn Stone, engaged in Torah and in halakhic instruction as is known; and it falls under the same category as the first itself, except that the one who seeks HaShem comes to them, and Samuel adds upon them, for he would circuit to instruct them. The third is the class that learns Torah for its own sake, for itself, to gain understanding and to comprehend words of pleasantness; it is superior to the first class that learns and teaches. The fourth is the class that supports the hands of the learners and goes out to their labor to find human sustenance." "And corresponding to the four classes he stated four metaphors. Corresponding to the class of Samuel he said <b>like streams they stretch out</b> — for they act like this stream, which carries its waters from place to place so that people may drink from it; so too this class, which labors on its own to give drink to those thirsty for living waters. And corresponding to the second class he said <b>like gardens beside a river</b> — like this garden, which is planted and whoever wishes to benefit may come and benefit, and also fruits are transported from it and feed those who are far away; so too they are a class planted in the Chamber of Hewn Stone, and whoever wishes to benefit may come and benefit, and also their fruits go far afield, for those who come there gain wisdom from them and go out to give drink to those thirsty for Torah, and thus it is written (Isaiah 2:3): ״כִּי מִצִּיּוֹן תֵּצֵא תוֹרָה״ etc. And corresponding to the third class — those who learn for themselves and lack the power to influence others — he said <b>like aloes HaShem has planted</b>, like Jacob, a wholesome man, dwelling in tents, learning for himself. And he used the language of planting, like this planting: the more it ages the more it adds roots and grip through its nourishment; so too learners of Torah — all the time one lengthens in the engagement of Torah, one's soul adds to take root in it. And corresponding to the fourth class he said <b>like cedars beside waters</b> — cedars alludes to the wealthy of the nation, people of stature, possessors of power and might in this world; but they do not produce fruits in Torah, for they do not engage in Torah learning." "<b>And already</b> I have written in several places that only the fruit of Torah is alone called fruit. And see what is explained in the Zohar (vol. 3, 104b) on the verse ״לֹא תַשְׁחִית אֶת עֵצָהּ וְגוֹ׳ מִמֶּנּוּ תֹאכֵל״ etc. (Deuteronomy 20:19), that the verse alludes to a master of Torah who produces fruit, etc. — it should be examined there. And he said <b>beside waters</b>, because their strength too is beside waters — which is Torah — for they support the hands of the men who learn Torah, which is compared to water, and they themselves benefit from the waters, for one who supports the hands of Torah learners shares reward with them as is known. And the intention of the entire statement is to say that all Israel stand rooted in the root of holiness — this Torah — even those engaged in the affairs of this world."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:49.426060528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}